As a Technical Lead, I completely respect this decision. By creating a separate endpoint and service method, you are following the Open-Closed Principle (Open for extension, Closed for modification).
If there is another screen in your frontend that strictly expects only PENDING requests, changing the existing method would break that screen. Creating a separate flow is the safest, most robust way to handle this.
Here is the complete, separate flow from top to bottom.
1. The Repository (CommonRequestRepository.java)
Add this new method. Keep your old findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusAndReqType method exactly as it is so you don't break existing code.
    // --- NEW METHOD FOR COMBINED STATUSES ---
    List<CommonReq> findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusInAndReqType(
            String creatorId, 
            List<RequestStatus> statuses, 
            RequestType reqType
    );

2. The Service Interface (RequestService.java)
Add the new contract to your interface.
    // --- NEW METHOD SIGNATURE ---
    List<CommonReq> getPendingAndQueueRequests(String userId, RequestType requestType);

3. The Service Implementation (RequestServiceImpl.java)
Implement the new method. This uses Arrays.asList() to pass both P and Q to the database query.
    // --- NEW METHOD IMPLEMENTATION ---
    @Override
    public List<CommonReq> getPendingAndQueueRequests(String userId, RequestType requestType) {
        if (requestType == null) {
            log.error("Request type is missing. Cannot fetch pending/queue requests for user: {}", userId);
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Request type is missing, Please provide correct request type to fetch requests."
            );
        }
        
        log.info("Fetching PENDING and QUEUE requests for user: {} with type: {}", userId, requestType);
        
        // Target both 'P' (Pending) and 'Q' (Queue) statuses
        List<RequestStatus> targetStatuses = Arrays.asList(
                RequestStatus.PENDING, 
                RequestStatus.QUEUE
        );

        return commonRequestRepository.findByCreatorIdNotAndReqStatusInAndReqType(
                userId, 
                targetStatuses, 
                requestType
        );
    }

4. The Controller (CommonRequestController.java)
Create a brand new endpoint. I recommend naming it /pending-queue-requests or /actionable-requests so it is clearly distinct from your existing /pending-requests endpoint.
    // --- NEW ENDPOINT ---
    @PostMapping("/pending-queue-requests")
    public List<CommonReq> getPendingAndQueueRequests(
            @RequestHeader("Authorization") String authorizationHeader,
            @RequestBody MyRequestFilterDto filterDto
    ) {
        String userId = jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(authorizationHeader);
        log.info("Received request for pending and queue data for the request type: {}", filterDto.getRequestType());
        
        // Call the new service method
        return requestService.getPendingAndQueueRequests(userId, filterDto.getRequestType());
    }

Why this is the perfect enterprise setup:
 * Zero Regression: Your existing /pending-requests API remains untouched and will continue working exactly as it did before.
 * Clean Data Fetching: The UI component handling LHO Transfers can now simply point to /pending-queue-requests to get both the rows waiting for the Checker and the rows waiting for Airflow, all in one efficient database hit.
